Test deck 1 for SMARDDA-PFC and example files for smpfcint.
It assumes that SMARDDA-PFC is installed in the directory
indicated by the shell environment variable $SMITER_DIR
and that this test-deck directory is $HSAUX. These might be set as
export SMITER_DIR=~/smardda/pfc
export HSAUX=~/smardda/smiteraux1
(If these environment variables are unset, tests.bash will make a guess
as to where smardda-pfc and the test-deck have been installed.)

The test-deck should be invoked using
./tests.bash
which should take less than 15 minutes on a high-end laptop or desktop. 
When no longer needed, the test-deck outputs can be removed using
./tests_clean.bash
When developing, it may be helpful instead to compare changes from one code version to the next.
The scripts
./tests_cp.bash
./tests_cpart.bash
will copy all or just the principal outputs to directory $HSAOLD which defaults to 
"$HSAUX"_old, i.e. ~/smardda/smiteraux1_old
when the outputs may be compared using 
./tests_diff.bash
./tests_difpart.bash

There are additional cases TCV_ben_skel and trid_skel
The idea is that these directories contain the "skeleton" files for the cases TCV_ben and trid,
so the first step is to make copies
cp -r TCV_ben_skel  TCV_ben; cp -r trid_skel  trid
then 
cd TCV_ben ; ./runRun.bash
cd ..
cd trid ; ./runtrid.sh
(The trid case requires 0.5GByte 3-D magnetic field files which have to be acquired separately.)

Data for the test-deck and smpfcint examples are in subdirectory Data,
which in turn has subdirectories
Equilibrium - eqdsk files, other equilibrium files, and 3-D vacuum field files
Geometry - contains .dat files, and ctl files for datvtk to generate geometry from points
(After test-deck execution, also contains log files from datvtk)
VTK - contains vtk files used by the test-deck, mostly produced via the CADfix route, plus
vtktfm ctl files for transforming and combining vtk files from CADfix and datvtk.
(After test-deck execution, also contains vtk files from datvtk and vtktfm, plus vtktfm
log files.)

Notes such as a modeller might jot down, describing the geometry, equilibrium and
other parameter settings required when using smpfcint to 
perform the Validation Problems Cases N=1, 3 and 4 (there is no Case 2), are in files
valN_notes.txt.

The conventions applying to the outputs are documented under
"Test-Deck" in the online SMARDDA-PFC documentation.

Each case is executed using one of the .sgo files in the $SMITER_DIR/Extras directory, specifically
tes.sgo, val1.sgo, val3.sgo and val4.sgo, with appropriate setting of the 
parameters flds, cal, etc., understanding of which should hopefully be
helped by the cases in the deck.  The important point for the user to note is that
the precise run performed depends both on the inline parameters and the 
contents of a set of skeleton .ctl files placed in the $SMITER_DIR/Extras directory.
The main inline parameters for input to .sgo are 
eqfile= *.eqdsk, full name of equilibrium file relative to ./Data/Equilibrium
eqid=MNEW8, short name for equilibrium, partly defines subdirectory name
gwall=wall, root name of silhouette file for first wall geometry helpful if $SMITER_DIR/Extras/wall.txt exists
gdatctl=dome_..+fw18+beancan, .dat file root name with instructions to generate .vtk (NOT tes.sgo, val1.sgo)
gdatshad=wdpdd06... .dat file, root-name to convert to shadow .vtk (NOT tes.sgo)
gdatres=wdpdd06.... dat file, root-name to convert to results .vtk (NOT tes.sgo)
gres=res12, name of results geometry file relative to ./Data/VTK, partly defines subdirectory name (tes.sgo)
=baf, name of .ctl file defining results geometry relative to ./Data/VTK, partly defines subdirectory name (NOT tes.sgo)
gshad=sha12, name of shadowing geometry file relative to ./Data/VTK, partly defines subdirectory name (tes.sgo)
=botsy, name of .ctl file defining shadowing geometry relative to ./Data/VTK, partly defines subdirectory name (NOT tes.sgo)
decl=0.01, set input variable  edgprofparameters - decay_length, lambda for exponential SOL 
pow=7.5, set input variable  edgprofparameters - power_loss, total power
vfld=testov5, root name of text file defining vacuum field (ONLY tes.sgo)
nzetp=1, sets input variable  beqparameters - beq_nzetap, no periodicity assumed if unity (NOT tes.sgo)
'+' is converted to space for onward processing so that eg. three .dat files are converted to .vtk for the above
setting of gdatcl

Inline parameters selecting the skeleton file name are:
flds=1 or 3, corresponding to input variable  beqparameters - beq_fldspec
cal=l or g, selects skeleton file name for local or global calculation
bdry=in, out or x, selects skeleton file name for limiter or detached discharge.
The set of skeleton .ctl files for doing outer-limited calculations in global coordinates is
MAG.ctl -> magtfm
RESq3out.ctl, SHADq3out.ctl ->geoq (q=U used by val1.sgo, q=V by val3/4.sgo)
HDS3.ctl -> hdsgen
POW3g.ctl ->powcal
and for detached calculations is
RES3x.ctl, SHAD3x.ctl ->  geoq
HDS3.ctl -> hdsgen
POW3g.ctl -> powcal
whereas for local coordinates, the files are 
WALL1in.ctl, RES1in.ctl SHAD1in.ctl -> geoq, inner limiter
RESq1out.ctl SHADq1out.ctl -> geoq, outer limiter (q=U used by val1.sgo, q=V by val3/4.sgo)
HDS1.ctl -> hdsgen
POW1l.ctl -> powcal
Use of local coordinates requires a certain amount of user skill in identifying
the mapped region, which depends on equilibrium.

For limiter calculations, the tes.sgo file assumes that the limit point is set by the first wall silhouette,
whereas val1/3/4.sgo assume that the limit point is set by the shadow geometry
